TITLE:: SDGGroup
summary:: a group track as in Ableton: orbits summed, a chain of effects, sends to the returns, a fader
categories:: Libraries>SuperDirtGUI
related:: Classes/SDGBusTrack, Classes/SDGMaster, Classes/SDGReturn, Classes/SuperDirtGUI

DESCRIPTION::
Orbits go into a group instead of the master, and what they sum to runs through a chain
of effects, sends to the returns, a fader with mute and solo, and out to the master.
Everything but the members is link::Classes/SDGBusTrack::.

code::
g = ~sdg.addGroup([0, 1, 2]);             // drums: orbits 0, 1 and 2
~sdg.addBusInsert(g, \compressor);        // one compressor on all three
~sdg.setOrbitDest(3, g.name);             // orbit 3 joins it
~sdg.setOrbitDest(3, \master);            // and leaves
g.solo = true;                            // hear its orbits and nothing else
g.solo = false;
~sdg.removeGroup(g);                      // its orbits go to the master again
::

An orbit joins by writing to it: its monitor's output bus is the group's in bus
(link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI#-setOrbitDest::), so everything of the orbit, effects and
fader included, is in what the group gets. The orbit's own sends to the returns stay;
the group's are on what the group plays, after its effects. Soloing a group solos its
orbits; soloing an orbit of a group keeps the group on, with the orbit alone in it.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
Made by link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI#-addGroup::, not by hand.
ARGUMENT:: sdg
the link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI::.
ARGUMENT:: name
its name, a Symbol.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: name
RETURNS:: its name: code::\g1::, code::\g2:: ...

METHOD:: shortName
RETURNS:: what a person calls it: "group 1".

METHOD:: members
RETURNS:: the indices of the orbits that go into it.
